{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1446 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "010f4d23e96e0008a1a7038256b83fd2efbc71d11c0d25bc0162b6ac121485b7" :edb/db-before-t 1445 :edb/db-after-t 1446 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534758 50 #inst "2026-10-08T06:01:58.593Z" 13194139534758 true] [17592186179360 1100 "Consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx = u - v - 2, \\qquad y = v - u + 5.\n$$\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix representing the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$\n\n- The transformation is invertible" 13194139534758 true] [17592186179360 1100 "Consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ defined as\n\n$x = u - v - 2, \\qquad y = v - u + 5$.\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix representing the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf T$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n2. $\\det(A) = 0$\n3. The transformation is invertible" 13194139534758 false] [17592186179360 1145 "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u$, and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$, and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The given transformation can be expressed as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}u - v - 2 \\\\ v - u + 5\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}u - v \\\\ -u + v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(A) \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(u) + \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(b)$.\nTherefore,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Statement II is true. Computing the determinant of $A$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\det(A) = 0$, the affine transformation is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534758 false] [17592186179360 1145 "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v-2 \\\\ v-u+5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ -u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\det(A) = 0,$ the affine transformation is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534758 true] [17592186179361 1100 "The matrix form of the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534758 true] [17592186179361 1100 "The matrix form of the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind $a+b+c$, given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534758 false] [17592186179361 1145 "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u$, and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$, and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $\\mathbf{T}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAu + b &= x \\\\\nAu &= x - b \\\\\nu &= A^{-1}(x - b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $\\mathbf T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{-1} &= (1)/((-4) \\cdot 7 - (-9) \\cdot 3)\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(u) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(A^{-1}) \\cdot (\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(x) - \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(b)) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c = -7 + 3 + 8 = 4\n$$" 13194139534758 false] [17592186179361 1145 "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $\\mathbf{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $\\mathbf T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-4)\\cdot 7-(-9)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c = -7 + 3 + 8 = 4.\n$$" 13194139534758 true] [17592186179362 1100 "The affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx = u - v + 2, \\qquad y = 4u - 5v + 9.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ of $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\nu = \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{a}x - y + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{b}, \\qquad v = \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{c}x - y + 1,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" 13194139534758 true] [17592186179362 1100 "The affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ is defined as\n\n$x = u - v + 2, \\qquad y = 4u - 5v + 9$.\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ of $\\mathbf T$ is\n$u = \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{a}x - y + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{b}, \\qquad v = \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{c}x - y + 1$,\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c$?" 13194139534758 false] [17592186179362 1145 "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u$, and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$, and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $\\mathbf{T}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAu + b &= x \\\\\nAu &= x - b \\\\\nu &= A^{-1}(x - b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}u - v + 2 \\\\ 4u - 5v + 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}u - v \\\\ 4u - 5v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(A) \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(u) + \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{-1} &= (1)/(1 \\cdot (-5) - (-1) \\cdot 4)\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(u) &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(A^{-1}) \\cdot (\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(x) - \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(b)) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x - 2 \\\\ y - 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\cdot (x - 2) + (-1) \\cdot (y - 9) \\\\ 4 \\cdot (x - 2) + (-1) \\cdot (y - 9)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}5x - y - 1 \\\\ 4x - y + 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu = \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{5}x - y + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{(-1)}, \\qquad v = \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{4}x - y + 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c = 5 + (-1) + 4 = 8\n$$" 13194139534758 false] [17592186179362 1145 "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $\\mathbf{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v+2 \\\\ 4u-5v+9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ 4u-5v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot (-5)-(-1)\\cdot 4}[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ y-9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot (x-2)+(-1)\\cdot (y-9) \\\\ 4\\cdot (x-2)+(-1)\\cdot (y-9)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5x-y-1 \\\\ 4x-y+1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu = \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{5}x - y + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{(-1)}, \\qquad v = \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{4}x - y + 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c = 5 + (-1) + 4 = 8.\n$$" 13194139534758 true] [17592186179365 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040441/assets/8797a50ddc0750a97190f96d8bc1cffe70c6ef9cc8e55a46f5a75d6b6702c81d.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nDescribe the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $\\mathbf{T}.$" 13194139534758 true] [17592186179365 1100 "Consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$\nDescribe the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3707/Source/Images/4252f221506b6465a4575a94777c7b53.png)" 13194139534758 false] [17592186179365 1145 "Recall that for any affine transformation\n$\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbf{u} \\longrightarrow A \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{b}$,\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) \\neq 0$, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) = 0$, we have two additional subcases:If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(T) &= \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix.\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-invertible and the image of the triangle under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a line segment." 13194139534758 false] [17592186179365 1145 "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbf{u} \\longrightarrow A \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{b},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) \\neq 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) = 0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf{T}$ is \n$[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-invertible and the image of the triangle under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a line segment." 13194139534758 true] [17592186179700 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308051 13194139534758 true] [17592186179700 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308058 13194139534758 true] [17592186179700 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308065 13194139534758 true] [17592186179700 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308072 13194139534758 true] [17592186179701 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308079 13194139534758 true] [17592186179701 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308086 13194139534758 true] [17592186179701 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308093 13194139534758 true] [17592186179701 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308100 13194139534758 true] [17592186179702 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308107 13194139534758 true] [17592186179702 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308114 13194139534758 true] [17592186179702 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308121 13194139534758 true] [17592186179702 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308128 13194139534758 true] [17592186179705 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308135 13194139534758 true] [17592186179705 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308142 13194139534758 true] [17592186179705 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308149 13194139534758 true] [17592186179705 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308156 13194139534758 true] [17592186179924 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534758 true] [17592186179924 1100 "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=u-v,\\,y=u+2v+1.\n$$\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix that represents the linear transformation associated with $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=0$\n- The transformation is invertible" 13194139534758 true] [17592186179924 1100 "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$x = u - v, y = u + 2v + 1$.\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix that represents the linear transformation associated with $T$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n2. $det(A) = 0$\n3. The transformation is invertible" 13194139534758 false] [17592186179924 1145 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ u+2v+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ u+2v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\ne [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1=3\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $det(A)\\ne 0,$ the affine transformation is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534758 true] [17592186179936 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534758 true] [17592186179936 1100 "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & 5 \\\\ b & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534758 true] [17592186179936 1100 "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}7 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFind $a + b + c$, given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation $T^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}a & 5 \\\\ b & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139534758 false] [17592186179936 1145 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 7-(-5)\\cdot (4)}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=3+4+3=10.\n$$" 13194139534758 true] [17592186179937 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534758 true] [17592186179937 1100 "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=5v-7u+3,\\,y=7v-10u+5.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=ax-5y+4,\\,v=bx+cy+5,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" 13194139534758 true] [17592186179937 1100 "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$x = 5v - 7u + 3, y = 7v - 10u + 5$.\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation $T^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n$u = ax - 5y + 4, v = bx + cy + 5$,\nwhat is the value of $a + b + c$?" 13194139534758 false] [17592186179937 1145 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5v-7u+3 \\\\ 7v-10u+5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7u+5v \\\\ -10u+7v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 5 \\\\ -10 & 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot (-7)-5\\cdot (-10)}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 \\\\ 10 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 \\\\ 10 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 \\\\ 10 & -7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 \\\\ 10 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-3 \\\\ y-5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7\\cdot (x-3)+(-5)\\cdot (y-5) \\\\ 10\\cdot (x-3)+(-7)\\cdot (y-5)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7x-5y+4 \\\\ 10x-7y+5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=7x-5y+4,\\,v=10x+(-7)y+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=7+10+(-7)=10.\n$$" 13194139534758 true] [17592186180020 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534758 true] [17592186180020 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040441/assets/3f8c1c9854ec54dd3caf4487328d3f3c35f2d69680a8fd1c13d28125dac77d03.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nWhat would be the image of the square shown above under the action of $T?$" 13194139534758 true] [17592186180020 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3707/Source/Images/q-162151.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhat would be the image of the square shown above under the action of $T$?" 13194139534758 false] [17592186180020 1145 "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-3)-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible and the image of the square under the action of $T$ is a line segment." 13194139534758 true] [17592186308051 1017 #edb/ref 17592186308052 13194139534758 true] [17592186308051 1089 "q-124210" 13194139534758 true] [17592186308051 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534758 true] [17592186308051 1100 "Which of the following affine transformations are invertible?\n\n- ${T}_{1}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}5u-2v-5 \\\\ 10u-4v+1\\end{aligned}]$\n- ${T}_{2}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u-v-4 \\\\ u+5v+7\\end{aligned}]$\n- ${T}_{3}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2u+4v-2 \\\\ 3u+6v+3\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139534758 true] [17592186308051 1113 #uuid "7c4490d1-fbae-525e-81b6-0d9b1dd1e143" 13194139534758 true] [17592186308051 1145 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThe transformation is invertible if the matrix $A$ is invertible. That is, if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{1}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{1}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5u-2v-5 \\\\ 10u-4v+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5u-2v \\\\ 10u-4v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 10 & -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 10 & -4\\end{aligned}|=-4\\cdot 5-(-2)\\cdot 10=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{1}$ is *not* invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{2}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{2}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v-4 \\\\ u+5v+7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ u+5v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}|=5\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 1=6\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{2}$ is invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{3}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{3}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2u+4v-2 \\\\ 3u+6v+3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2u+4v \\\\ 3u+6v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}|=6\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 3=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{3}$ is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{2}$ only.\"" 13194139534758 true] [17592186308052 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308053 13194139534758 true] [17592186308052 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308054 13194139534758 true] [17592186308052 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308055 13194139534758 true] [17592186308052 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308056 13194139534758 true] [17592186308052 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308057 13194139534758 true] [17592186308052 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534758 true] [17592186308052 1041 "selection" 13194139534758 true] [17592186308052 1104 #edb/ref 17592186308053 13194139534758 true] [17592186308052 1110 #uuid "d4a0113b-5818-5f93-a5a3-c253e864b429" 13194139534758 true] [17592186308053 1034 "${T}_{2}$ only" 13194139534758 true] [17592186308053 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534758 true] [17592186308053 1111 #uuid "0e0f6c23-8c5a-58ec-8eea-2bd9a8ac9f69" 13194139534758 true] [17592186308054 1034 "${T}_{1}$ only" 13194139534758 true] [17592186308054 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534758 true] [17592186308054 1111 #uuid "a7599421-2a18-5beb-a436-488f533e00bf" 13194139534758 true] [17592186308055 1034 "${T}_{2}$ and ${T}_{3}$ only" 13194139534758 true] [17592186308055 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534758 true] [17592186308055 1111 #uuid "815cac7c-f3fd-51cc-9d9f-3d2675fb67ca" 13194139534758 true] [17592186308056 1034 "${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{2}$ only" 13194139534758 true] [17592186308056 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534758 true] [17592186308056 1111 #uuid "a39441da-29ec-560b-83dc-8f18218ca9b7" 13194139534758 true] [17592186308057 1034 "${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{3}$ only" 13194139534758 true] [17592186308057 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534758 true] 